#let abstract-content = [
Exergames and automation for rehabilitation have been shown to be effective in providing personalized rehabilitation to patients with various conditions. However, therapists' sense of agency in relation to AI-supported computational systems for exergame-based rehabilitation remains largely underexplored. With the aim of exploring this gap, the study investigates how therapists reason about their sense of agency, and how agency could be distributed between therapists and system across the rehabilitation process, through a participatory Research through Design (RtD) approach by employing two main methods: (1) a contextual inquiry conducted through a case study (Brain Stimulation) including interviews and observation, and (2) individual participatory speculative co-design sessions. Eight participants in total, with an average of over 13 years of relevant experience were involved in the study. The results indicate that, under certain conditions, therapists' would still retain a sense of agency even when AI took agency over operational control, provided that their responsibility and care over their patient is not threatened. These conditions shifted the agency distribution requirements across the different phases of rehabilitation. Collectively, this study contributes a phase-dependant account of human-AI co-performance in an exergame-based rehabilitation system.
]